WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
Make a secretory protein in a test tube with no membranes.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.820
It comes out longer than the protein the cell secretes.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:15.320
Why? The extra piece is an address.

4
00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:21.280
The cell reads it while the chain is still on the ribosome, then cuts it off once

5
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.420
the protein has arrived.

6
00:00:23.283 --> 00:00:28.323
Almost every protein in a cell starts on a ribosome in the cytosol.

7
00:00:28.823 --> 00:00:31.483
That is the fluid outside the organelles.

8
00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:36.963
A signal sequence is a stretch of amino acids that carries sorting information.

9
00:00:37.743 --> 00:00:39.583
Think of a zip code printed on the parcel.

10
00:00:40.563 --> 00:00:43.583
A protein with no signal stays in the cytosol.

11
00:00:44.317 --> 00:00:50.517
For the ER, the address is a hydrophobic stretch, read while the chain is being

12
00:00:50.517 --> 00:00:56.217
made. A particle called SRP binds it as it leaves the ribosome.

13
00:00:57.037 --> 00:01:01.157
In a wheat germ test tube, SRP halts the growing chain.

14
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:05.757
Then it brings the ribosome to its receptor on the ER membrane.

15
00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:11.607
With GTP bound, SRP and its receptor hold together.

16
00:01:12.287 --> 00:01:17.647
They pass the ribosome to the Sec61 channel, which is the pore into the ER.

17
00:01:18.807 --> 00:01:23.427
GTP hydrolysis then separates them, and the chain threads through.

18
00:01:24.407 --> 00:01:29.267
On the lumenal side, an enzyme called signal peptidase cuts off the signal.

19
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:32.387
That is the extra piece the test tube left on.

20
00:01:33.217 --> 00:01:39.557
Mitochondria, chloroplasts and peroxisomes mostly read chains that are already

21
00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:46.557
finished. A peroxisomal address is usually a tripeptide at the carboxyl end, such as

22
00:01:46.557 --> 00:01:53.897
SKL. Join the first 53 residues of a yeast mitochondrial precursor to a mouse

23
00:01:53.897 --> 00:01:57.257
enzyme, and the fusion enters yeast mitochondria.

24
00:01:57.257 --> 00:02:03.237
Add SKL to the end of a bacterial enzyme, and it enters peroxisomes in mammalian

25
00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:04.237
cells.

26
00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.880
So the address is in the protein itself.

27
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:11.020
It is read by a receptor on the organelle.

28
00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.620
A protein with no signal stays in the cytosol by default.

29
00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.060
A signal attached to a protein that normally stays there can send it elsewhere.

30
00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:27.380
If moving a stretch of sequence moves the protein, that stretch is sufficient to sort it.
